.he "strings (1)                  8/16/26                  strings (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
strings
- print the printable strings in a binary file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
strings [ - ] [ -o ] [ -# ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Strings reads a binary file and writes every sequence of printing
characters it finds that is long enough.  A string is four or more
printing characters, and is shown one to a line; a longer run is not
broken up.
.sp
When the file is a micronix object file, strings reads only the text
and data, the parts a program keeps its literals in, and skips the
header in front and the symbols behind.  The - flag makes it search
the whole file, bookkeeping and all.  With a plain data file that has
no object header, everything is searched either way.
.sp
With no file, strings reads the standard input.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-	Search the whole file rather than only the program parts.

.ti -10
-o	Precede each string by its offset in the file, in octal.

.ti -10
-#	Take # as the minimum string length instead of 4.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The port inverted one assumption the original carried.  On the VAX
the compiler put a program's strings in its initialized data, so
strings skipped the text and searched only there.  The micronix
compiler keeps them in text, so this strings searches text and data
together and skips only the header and symbols - the same idea,
"read the program, not the bookkeeping", turned to fit the machine.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
od (1)
